this is sandman being made for people  who love sandman by people who love  sandman we're getting into all the  challenges and behind the scenes visions  of everything that brought this  incredible series to the silver screen  i'm here at shepparton studios and i'm  getting to see what happens when you  bring dreams to life number one tom  sturridge who plays dream was basically  naked for the entirety of the first  episode the way he was first introduced  to his fellow castmates was naked  climbing into a glass sphere and because  of the way the sphere was designed they  couldn't open it super easily so he  would be in there by himself for hours  at a time one aspect he loved about that  though was how that really helped him  get into character in that silence you  can only ever really believe yourself to  be a character number two speaking of  getting into character one thing tom  really struggled with was being  introduced as a protagonist who doesn't  speak or even move much for that matter  it was incredibly important to me to  somehow articulate this otherworldliness  he had a lot to convey with just his  body and facial expressions while using  no words he needed to show the audience  that he wasn't human he was an eternal  being who spent his every fiber holding  all these universes dreams together  that's a lot of pressure with no  dialogue i did work very hard hopefully  to create an image that would tell an  audience that story with no words number  three the realm of lucifer morningstar  the vfx team spent more time on this  episode than any other episode there are  so many incredible practical effects the  gate for example is an actual sculpture  at first they didn't even know if they  should show the set on screen bfx  supervisor ian markowitz tells dan of  geek anything that we show of lucifer's  palace is going to be disappointing no  matter what it is no matter how cool it  is whatever we show is going to be a  little underwhelming but it turned out  to be their most rewarding episode they  ended up utilizing real-life images like  the vatican and a specific cathedral in  milan as inspiration number four so much  was actually built practically our art  department are set decorators our  production designers they are wizards it  was really important to neil gaiman and  the rest of the show's creators that  whenever they could create or build  something they would do it there's  actually not a ton of cgi in the show  compared to what you might think they  even had a real raven on set it didn't  speak unfortunately but tom did get to  interact with a real live bird having a  raven there was amazing just something  like you're talking it's like  number five who else is a fan of  despair's crocs for starters jenna  coleman's character joanna went through  almost a dozen different costume  iterations before they landed on her  iconic trench coat look also i got  really over excited about having a  crucifix in my boot for tom sturvig's  costume design they also went through a  few different looks at first he just  wanted to be as true to the comic book  character as he could get and i wanted  my hair to be wild and i wanted my eyes  to be as dark as night but obviously  that's not the dream we ended up with  neil will agree with me is i looked  ridiculous they ended up sticking with  tom's more natural luck as they found  that to be a better fit for the screen  number six we've gotta talk about  matthew considering they really wanted  to take a mostly practical effect with  this show they definitely wanted their  cgi to be as top-notch as possible ian  markowitz shared his story in creating  matthew with den of geek it is probably  the most successful cg feather bird that  i've seen on film or television and  that's saying a lot because we had a  photo real aspiration for something that  was known the thing was either going to  look like a real bird or it wasn't  matthew not only had to look real but he  had to fly move and interact with the  rest of the cast considering he was once  human himself they had to create facial  expressions that resembled natural human  emotion not a simple task  number seven we're all familiar with the  famous john constantine that's the  reason why he's not in the show neil  thought he'd been represented enough in  the media so he pitched the idea to  allen about turning him into joanna we  both thought about jenna and jenna's  voice and that went a long way just in  terms of my conception of who she was  they wanted to do a fresh spin on the  character so they took what they loved  about jon and brought new life into it  and we just had a lot of fun i think um  on all fronts number eight there were  several sandman movie and television  pitches that didn't work over the years  neil gaiman always felt passionate about  bringing sandman to life but only if it  could be done in the right way he told  ign i didn't care about it getting made  i would so much rather that it never got  made then a bad version would get made  even a director he really respects eric  kripke pitched a sandman series for  network tv in 2010 neil gaiman had many  reservations about that at the time the  damage that you had to do to sandman to  put it on network tv 15 years ago and in  the kind of budgets and the way that you  could do it just meant it wasn't sandman  number nine if you'd read the comics  you'd know how well they pulled this off  when filming the show they really wanted  as many scenes as they could manage to  be like you pulled it straight off the  page what was really thrilling was  remembering how accurately some of those  frames were replicated they always had a  copy of the graphic novel right next to  the camera that was so important to us  when we were making number ten ian  markowitz definitely had his hands full  here something really beautiful but also  incredibly difficult about the show was  that every episode is different from the  one before it with dream being the only  thing holding the timeline together ian  shares his experience working on the  show's wide range of sets it's not a  procedural where we're doing similar  things next week there's always  something that's going to be different  about the next episode and dream may not  be on screen for every frame of the show  but he is always going to be at the  heart of things  number 11 not even his original comics  or panels from 1989 we're talking about  the scripts he wrote for artists before  any of that even got onto the page when  ian markowitz was brought on as the vfx  supervisor he found the project to be a  bit daunting at first because of how  well known the world of sandman already  was he describes having been given  neil's notes as being gifted gold he  explains i thought that starting with  neil's notes was the perfect way for us  to think about adapting the show because  sometimes the comic book panel is very  comic booky and it has that wild quirky  zany quality which i don't think the  show has all of that much  number 12 not only did tom sturridge go  through it with his look but he also had  to mentally prepare to become the  eternal being that is morpheus neil gave  him some pertinent advice on how dream  carries himself he has thought every  thought that could ever be thought or  could ever be conceived of with every  line he said he had to utilize his voice  to convey that message to us therefore  when he speaks  it should feel as if it's etched in  stone number 13 if anyone can do it neil  gaiman can neil gaiman what's the key  the sandman comics were first released  over three decades ago but the cult  following has remained extremely  dedicated it makes sense that a casual  dinner conversation between neil gaiman  alan heinberg and david goyer would lead  to a netflix pitch three days later  honestly that sort of felt like okay  well it feels like it would be a tv  first season number fourteen they  actually had the cast before the writing  was even finished allen heinberg was  extremely grateful that the sandman  already had such a massive following  they were able to cast for it pretty  much immediately having the cast at hand  was highly preferred as allen likes to  write with the actor's voice in mind one  of the characters that went through the  most transformation on the page was  corinthian neil and i decided you know  what we're going to embrace everything  that void is boyd holbrook being cast  completely changed the way they  approached the character and there was a  version of that within with everybody in  the cast number 15 there has to be at  least one there are so many incredible  characters with stories worth following  when talking about the end of sandman it  makes it much easier for the showrunners  to see the end when you have source  material you're going off of but  something unknown that excites them are  all the potential series they could  create from other characters there are  death miniseries we'd love to explore  doing there are other characters worthy  of having their own adventures so after  going through the practical set designs  comic book replicas costumes and neil  gaiman's vintage sandman scripts were  still completely floored by all the  incredible talent and dedication it took  to bring this series to life what were  you most excited to see on screen  [Music]
 

